# Part 2. Understand and reshape the data

## Step 4. Understand wide versus long

The following is an **illustration only**, not the actual life expectancy file.

| Country | 2010 | 2020 |
|:--|--:|--:|
| Country A | 65 | 70 |
| Country B | 72 | 76 |

This is **wide**: each year is a separate column. The same information in **long** format would look like this:

| Country | Year | Life expectancy |
|:--|--:|--:|
| Country A | 2010 | 65 |
| Country A | 2020 | 70 |
| Country B | 2010 | 72 |
| Country B | 2020 | 76 |

Long data are often easier to filter, group, plot, and summarize across time. Wide data can be useful for reports and side-by-side comparisons.

::: {.callout-note title="Method spotlight: `pd.melt()`"}
`pd.melt()` reshapes selected **column headers into row values**. Consider the purpose of `id_vars` (columns that identify a unit), `value_vars` (columns to unpivot), `var_name` (new column containing former headers), and `value_name` (new column containing measurements). Consult the [pandas documentation](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.melt.html) as needed.
:::

